            <ArticleTitle>Refractoriness to exercise challenge: a review of the mechanisms old and new.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>329-45, viii</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.iac.2013.02.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0889-8561(13)00012-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This article discusses the available literature on refractoriness in exercise-induced bronchoconstriction, namely, a decrease in airway responsiveness with repeated exercise challenges. The mechanisms of this naturally occurring protective feature is unknown. Reviewing previous studies together with findings in more recent studies, the authors propose desensitization of the G protein-coupled cysteinyl leukotriene receptor1 as the mechanism of refractoriness and that this desensitization occurs as a result of interplay between leukotrienes and prostaglandins.</AbstractText>
